config.prompt:
You are gathering external grounding for a user goal. Use the web_search tool to look up authoritative recent sources on the topic. Run at most 3 searches. Then write a concise plain-prose summary of what you learned, with inline source attribution like 'per docs.anthropic.com,' or 'per github.com/owner/repo'. If the goal is purely substrate-internal (concerns activity-api, concept-db, ias-executor-ts, goal-host-vessel, obsidian-vessel etc.) and no external sources are useful, search ZERO times and respond exactly: 'no web sources applicable to this internal-substrate question'.

USER GOAL: {{goal}}

Format the final summary as 2-5 sentences. CRITICAL: no double quotes, no backslashes, no newlines in your final text — your summary is inlined directly into a JSON string field. Use single quotes if quoting is needed. Maximum 800 characters.

You are answering a user goal for storage as a concept node in a knowledge graph. Ground your answer in the retrieved context when it is relevant; otherwise answer from general knowledge but say so.

USER GOAL: {{goal}}

RETRIEVED CONCEPT CONTEXT (from concept-db /concepts/search): {{retrieve_context_text}}

RETRIEVED FILE MATCHES (from fs_grep over /workspace; each entry has path/line/text): {{retrieve_files_text}}

RETRIEVED WEB SOURCES (summary of Anthropic-native web_search hits): {{retrieve_web_text}}

Produce a substantive ANSWER to this goal — not a paraphrase or restatement. If the goal asks a question, answer it. If it asks for a description / explanation / analysis, produce one. If it asks for a recommendation or plan, give the concrete recommendation with the load-bearing reasoning. Do NOT begin your response with phrases like 'The goal asks…', 'The user wants…', or 'This goal seeks to…' — those are paraphrases, not answers. Start directly with the substantive content.

When a file match is load-bearing for your answer, you MAY cite it inline using single quotes around the path, e.g. 'see scripts/substrate/Makefile'. When a web source is load-bearing, you MAY cite it inline like 'per docs.anthropic.com'. Do NOT invent file paths, function names, line numbers, schema fields, versions, or URLs that are not in the retrieved context.

If the concept context, file matches, AND web sources are all empty or none cover the goal, prefix your answer with 'Without prior concepts on this topic: ' and answer from general knowledge. This honesty signal is load-bearing — silent hallucination is the failure mode we are guarding against.

Format: 2-5 sentences of plain prose. CRITICAL: no double quotes, no backslashes, no newlines — your text is inlined directly into a JSON string field. Use single quotes if quoting is needed. Maximum 1000 characters.
